 good deal thinking  2500 dollars a month is a good deal is  the problem according to ndp leader jug  meet singh he claims rental prices have  risen four thousand dollars per year  since the liberals took power and singh  says it's because of certain loopholes  he plans to close  the cmhc is being used right now by  large corporations  to to get cmhc backed loans that allow  them to purchase buildings and units  that they then renovate and effectively  renovate their  their occupants  singh is also promising to hand out up  to five thousand dollars a year in  rental supports for families  the liberals haven't released their full  housing platform yet but the spring  budget built on the 2017 strategy with a  2.5 billion dollar commitment to create  thirty five hundred affordable housing  units the ndp is pledging to build five  hundred thousand affordable homes within  ten years while the conservatives plan  is to build one million homes over three  years but nothing specific on affordable  units we fought for people in the  pandemic and we're going to fight for  people in the recovery  the affordable homes singh is promising  would likely be built by those same  developers the ndp says are taking  advantage of cmhc loopholes when pressed  on that point  singh softened his language somewhat we  know we'll have to work with private  developers but we want to make sure that  the priority is not on those developers  it's on people and we can incentivize  and encourage developers to work in a  way that actually benefits canadians  sunday will be a little more somber for  singh he's scheduled to attend just one  event in the morning where he'll help to  mark the 10-year anniversary of the  passing of former mvp leader jack layton  jeff mike look at her in toronto thanks  mike jugmeet singh and the four other  national party leaders will square off  in a debate in both english and french  early next month but one face that you  will not see on the stage this time  around is people's party of canada  leader maxine bernier the leaders  debates commission confirmed today that  bernie's party did not meet its criteria  the ppc didn't win a single seat in the  last election despite running candidates  in every single riding across the  country the party that bernier founded  received just 1.6 of the vote that's  below the 4 threshold and recent opinion  polls suggest its national support  remains below 4  the french debate will be on september  the 8th at 8 pm eastern and the english  program will air september 9th at 9 pm  eastern both will be at the canadian  museum of history in gatineau quebec  still ahead the race for 24 sussex one  week into the election campaign who  appears the most popular potential prime  minister  plus breakthrough infections what are  the chances of a fully vaccinated  canadian catching covid19  welcome back liberal leader justin  trudeau wrapped up the first week of the  federal election campaign with a clear  advantage according to new polling from  ipsos canadians trust the incumbent  liberals on three of the top five  election issues health care the pandemic  and climate change the conservatives and  leader aaron o'toole are trusted most on  the economy while jugg jugmeat sings ndp  has the edge when it comes to the issue  of affordability with a campaign lasting  just five weeks there's only a brief  window for opposition leaders to  persuade voters it's time for a change  eric sorenson has more  justin trudeau began the election  campaign with a head start his presence  for 18 months during the pandemic all  but shut out the opposition leaders on  the national stage he's just better  known than his political rivals i'm  asking you to stand with us  vote for real ambitious leadership it  shows in polling by ipsos as the  election kicked off trudeau was seen as  the best candidate for prime minister by  39 percent of canadians well ahead of  conservative leader aaron o'toole at 25  percent and jagmeet singh at 23.  compared with voter intentions trudeau  runs slightly ahead of his party as does  singh o'toole lags a little behind the  conservative brand as the campaign  kicked off the incumbent prime  minister's always in polling i've been  doing this for about 30 years always  have an advantage whenever you ask  anything about leadership canada's  conservatives will stand with those who  suffered during the downturn an election  campaign though helps to level the  playing field the opposition in  particular o'toole and singh are now  getting more media coverage and a closer  look from canadians at their plans for  the country while justin trudeau has  made a lot of announcements and done a  lot for show it really hasn't improved  people's lives a big part of what this  campaign is going to be about is  building familiarity for the new  opposition party leader um aaron o'toole  and for singh he's still got a lot of  blanks to fill in for the canadian  public as well  incumbency however is a two-edged sword  familiarity can breed trust for some  voters and mistrust for others  assessing party leader attributes  trudeau leads the others on who  canadians think will keep their election  promises and who's best to manage during  tough economic times but he also leads  as the party leader more canadians think  has a hidden agenda and the one who will  say anything to get elected  [Applause]  so the opposition chips away at the  front runner has he done anything he's  been in power for six years if the  numbers don't move during the course of  the campaign then the opposition parties  haven't done their job it is a steep  climb for opposition leaders who've been  largely sidelined by the pandemic to  present themselves and their platforms  they have barely four weeks left to  become better known and trusted by  canadian voters eric sorensen global  news toronto  still ahead a fully vaccinated toronto  woman's battle with covid19  you're watching global national  welcome back even if you're double  vaccinated that doesn't mean you're not  at risk of catching covid19 so-called  breakthrough cases are on the rise as  canada faces a fourth wave and experts  warn those cases will grow as the delta  variant surges but there's also some  good news jamie morocco explains  when janice singer returned from a trip  to mexico she says she didn't feel very  well imagine if you were to have like a  regular bold that's very light but a  test at pearson international airport  confirmed it wasn't a common cold the  double vaccinated torontonian caught it  was coveted 19. i got the vaccine and  then i went to a place that had very  very high  covet transmission  and i did nothing to protect myself  health experts say the highly  transmissible delta variant is making  breakthrough cases like singers more  common we see more and more people that  they are even with two vaccines uh even  talking about pfizer  they can be vaccinated  infected and also they can transmit the  disease by the end of july in ontario  for example out of roughly 8.5 million  fully vaccinated people nearly 2 000  were infected with covid19  and in iceland a shining example of  vaccination rates at 70 of its total  population fully inoculated between  mid-july and early august 77 of  infections were in people who were fully  vaccinated the silver lining almost all  had mild symptoms the vaccines prevent  you from getting disease they don't make  you impervious to infections infectious  disease expert donald vince says the  lack of severe outcome is why  vaccinations are so important your body  will be able to respond to that  infection and neutralize the virus much  sooner than somebody who's not  vaccinated and why singer is relieved  she got the shot a hundred percent i'm  happy that i got vaccinated new data out  of the u.s this week revealed certain  covet 19 shots don't offer long-term  protection and why health officials  there are recommending boosters for all  american adults here health experts say  we should stay focused on cobit 19  safety measures at least until globally  vaccination rates rise jamie morocco  global news toronto  congratulations priscilla garnier next  meet the woman chosen to lead team  canada into the tokyo paralympic games  [Music]  welcome back the paralympic flame has  now been lit in tokyo and ahead of the  opening ceremony team canada has just  revealed who will carry the country's  flag  it's been my dream to compete in the  paralympics ever since i was a  ten-year-old child and to be given the  honor of flagra is beyond anything i  could have imagined i really hope that  for the kids  living with disabilities this inspires  them to never stop dreaming to never  listen to somebody who tells them no and  to stand together with people who  believe in them their fellow  paralympians  that's pera judo champion priscilla  gagne the 35 year old from sarnia  ontario ranks number two in the world in  women's 52 kilograms this is her second  paralympics she's hoping to beat her  fifth place finish from rio back in  2016. the opening ceremonies kick off on  tuesday congratulations and that is  global national for this saturday night  i'm jeff semple tonight your canada is  the city of new westminster british  columbia  i'll be back here with you tomorrow have  a great night  [Music]  you
 

